# Per-cell benchmark checkpoints. These carry the raw per-star probability vectors for
# every method in every cell (several MB), which is the same reason `*.npz` is ignored at
# the repo root: bulk arrays stay out of git, and anything a paper quotes must live in the
# committed JSON sidecar instead.
#
# The checkpoints are regenerable by re-running
# `benchmark_erotica_vs_asteca.py`, and the sidecar is regenerable from a checkpoint with
# `benchmark_finalise.py <out>.json`.
*.cells.jsonl

# The two assembled benchmark sidecars, which BREAK the contract stated just above and are
# ignored for that reason rather than as an exception to it. Every other sidecar in this
# directory is 1-14 KB; these are 3040 KB and 776 KB, because `benchmark_finalise.py` copies
# per-cell arrays into the assembled file instead of reducing them. So they are checkpoints
# wearing a sidecar's name, and `check-added-large-files` was right to reject them.
#
# Regenerate: re-run `benchmark_erotica_vs_asteca.py`, then
# `benchmark_finalise.py <name>.json`. The quotable numbers are the summariser's output
# (`benchmark_erotica_vs_asteca_summarise.py`) and are written up in
# `~/phd/agent-findings/benchmark-results.md` and `~/phd/papers/P02.md`.
#
# TODO: make benchmark_finalise.py emit a reduced sidecar, then un-ignore these.
benchmark_erotica_vs_asteca.json
benchmark_threeway_adaptive.json

# Raw per-fit rows from the HDBSCAN config screen: 11.5 MB for 2700 fits, i.e. the same
# checkpoint-wearing-a-sidecar's-name defect as the two files above. Regenerable with
# `hdbscan_config_sweep.py`. The pattern IS fixed here rather than only flagged: that script now
# also writes `hdbscan_config_sweep.summary.json` (278 KB, committed), which carries mean/sd/n/
# min/max per (factor, level, variant) for the eleven metrics a conclusion is quoted from. Quote
# the summary; the raw file exists only to recompute it.
hdbscan_config_sweep.json

# Cached VizieR pulls. Same contract as `hr24_clusters.ecsv`: the fetch script, not the
# file, is the source of truth, so a stale catalogue cannot silently persist.
# `hr24_members.parquet` is 37 MB / 1291929 rows, regenerable with
# `fetch_hr24_members.py`; `.chunks/` is that script's per-page resume cache.
hr24_members.parquet
hr24_members.chunks/
